# The Optimization Problem

* Minimizing a Function

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}

- @Goal@: Minimize scalar function $J(\vw)$ of $P$-variables $\vw$
  - E.g., in-sample error
    $E_{in}(\vw) \defeq \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N e(h_{\vw}(\vx_i), y_i)$, the average
    point-wise error of hypothesis $h_{\vw}$ over $N$ training examples
    $(\vx_i, y_i)$

- @Solutions@
  1. _Analytical approach_
     - Impose the gradient of $J(\vw)$ to equal 0
     - Find a closed-form solution for $\vw^*$
  2. _Numerical approach_
     - Use an iterative method to update $\vw$ to reach the minimum value of $J(\vw)$
       - E.g., gradient descent
     - It works even if there is an analytical solution

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L02.4.Gradient_descent_2.png)

